\title{Divide and conquer introduction}
\date{2026-10-19T13:35:21Z}
\tag{algorithms}
\tag{draft}

\import{algorithms-macros}

\p{\em{Divide and conquer} is a typical problem solving strategy that works as follows:

\ol{
  \li{Break up the input into (smaller) subproblems.}
  \li{Solve the subproblems recursively.}
  \li{Combine the subproblem solutions into an overall solution.}
}

When it works, this is a beautiful technique and very amenable to analysis:

\ul{
  \li{To \em{implement} a divide-and-conquer strategy, we can use \em{recursion}.}
  \li{To \em{prove the correctness} of a divide-and-conquer strategy, we can use \em{proof by induction}.}
  \li{To \em{analyze the asymptotic runtime} of a divide-and-conquer strategy, we can use \em{recurrence relations} and the \em{Master Theorem}.}   % XXX link to Master Theorem
}
}
